description:
Fourth blind stroke-sculpt run on the skull, using the tools MESH-80 and MESH-86 added. It tests whether those tools let a sculptor working only from reference pictures get closer than run 3 (MESH-79) did. The new tools:
- strokes stored as surface curves on the base, applied in one pass;
- sculpt-sync, so a stroke drawn in one view shows and moves in all 8;
- data-radius and data-depth in world units;
- the per-stroke report, bowl fills, and silhouette strokes that move an outline;
- a symmetric silhouette_hull base and outline-compare.

Stacked on mesh-86 (itself on mesh-80); the sculpt lives in resources/dsl/skull4/.

Stage 1 (orchestrator): new jittered references; the base comes from trace-outlines into silhouette_hull(symmetry="x"), volume_to_mesh, keep_largest_part, smooth_mesh and quad_layout, saved as base.ply. It is checked against the traced outlines with outline-compare, against a threshold set beforehand.

Stage 2: the sculptor works in rounds; a judge that sees only numbers scores each round and alone ends the run. The rule: a round counts as a change when it beats the best so far by at least 0.5% (relative) in chamfer or image distance; stop after three consecutive rounds without a change, or when chamfer reaches twice the floor; no round cap. Image distance is rendered with the headlight on.

Known gaps going in:
- MESH-78's fold and crossing limiter is not in the one-pass code, so a mesh can still turn inside out.
- quad_layout folds about 20 faces of a fold-free hull (PATCH-128).

Record every friction the sculptor hits.
